Or we can see it the other way round; using speed boots gives you the
tactical flexibility to go burdened while (boots of) jumping restricts
you. And jumping from spell is much more convenient (you can even jump
if strained, it seems).

> Remaining unburdened is more important than either speed or jumping.
> [...]

Yes, mostly. But mind that in tough cases you can unburden yourself
in a single turn and still have speed (or jumping) from the boots.

You can take advantage of speed continuously, without need to bother
about food or explicit "activation". Speed is a permanently useful
property, jumping is useful just in specific cases. That's why my
choice clearly goes for speed boots than for jumping boots; they're
more generally useful (and unrestricted).

>>      With the right spells, you can be both very fast and able to
>> jump at the same time, having the best of both worlds.

Indeed. But the jumping spell is IMO tactically better than the speed
spell; I prefer permanent speed from boots, and PW consuming jumping
for the few tight situations, and not the just temporary haste self
spell and permanent jumping property from the boots.

> Yep.  In practice, I often have haste self or blessed potions of speed
> to use on Astral/Air/Sanctum/etc.  If not, though, I'd much rather
> have only jumping boots than only speed boots--and if I'm going to
> burn a wish on boots it'll be for jumping.

I'd rather wish for the spellbook of jumping (for the respective classes).

BTW, it's worthwhile to note that besides the teleport-others/jumping
